WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.481
Imagine a closed piece of string. You can bend and stretch it, but never cut it or pass

2
00:00:05.481 --> 00:00:10.773
one part through another. A knot diagram shows its shadow, with a small gap wherever

3
00:00:10.773 --> 00:00:13.104
one strand passes underneath another.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.685
Now stand the knot in space and count its humps, the local high points.

5
00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:22.518
The bridge number is the smallest number of humps you can achieve by reshaping it.

6
00:00:22.518 --> 00:00:26.820
This simple height sketch explains the count; it is not our knot diagram.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.365
One arrangement with four humps proves that four are enough.

8
00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:34.420
It does not prove that three are impossible.

9
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.071
And thirteen crossings in a diagram does not mean thirteen bridges.

10
00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:42.056
Crossings and humps count different things.

11
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:46.858
Our example is the knot labelled thirteen n one thousand and five.

12
00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:50.477
The pinned table allowed either three or four bridges.

13
00:00:50.477 --> 00:00:56.041
We will see why four suffice, and why every attempt to get down to three must fail.

14
00:00:56.433 --> 00:01:01.105
To rule out every possible drawing, we need something that survives reshaping.

15
00:01:01.105 --> 00:01:04.100
Imagine travelling in the space around the string.

16
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.694
A tiny loop going once around a strand is called a meridian.

17
00:01:07.694 --> 00:01:10.689
You can join loop journeys and run them backwards.

18
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:16.919
The knot group records how such journeys combine, allowing deformations that never cross

19
00:01:16.919 --> 00:01:22.571
the string. Meridional rank means the fewest meridian journeys needed to generate all

20
00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:27.027
of them. We will prove that at least four are needed for this knot.

21
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.442
A presentation with b bridges supplies b meridians that generate the group.

22
00:01:32.442 --> 00:01:35.399
So meridional rank is at most bridge number.

23
00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:39.970
If three meridians are impossible, three bridges are impossible too.

24
00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.264
The next step turns journeys into an easier test.

25
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:47.295
Put eight numbered dots on the screen, zero through seven.

26
00:01:47.295 --> 00:01:50.641
They are bookkeeping objects, not points on the knot.

27
00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:53.355
Assign a shuffling action to each meridian.

28
00:01:53.355 --> 00:01:58.153
A valid assignment must respect every crossing relation in the knot diagram.

29
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:04.014
Here is one action. Swap zero with one, and at the same time swap two with three.

30
00:02:04.014 --> 00:02:09.698
Leave the other four dots alone. This is called a double transposition: two disjoint

31
00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:13.893
swaps. Doing it twice returns every dot to its starting place.

32
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:17.529
The test deliberately forgets some knot information.

33
00:02:17.529 --> 00:02:22.798
But any meridians generating the entire knot group must still generate its shuffling

34
00:02:22.798 --> 00:02:28.569
actions. That lets a small finite model rule out a proposed generator count for the original

35
00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:28.883
knot.

36
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:34.859
Zoom into the first saved crossing. The incoming understrand swaps zero with one and

37
00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:39.585
two with three. The overstrand swaps zero with one and three with four.

38
00:02:39.585 --> 00:02:43.979
The outgoing understrand must carry the related action shown here.

39
00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:50.167
The rule is called conjugation. You can understand it as renaming the points in an action.

40
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:54.679
Use the overstrand shuffle to rename both ends of every incoming swap.

41
00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:56.871
We can do this one pair at a time.

42
00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:01.469
The pair zero, one becomes one, zero: still the same swap.

43
00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:04.223
The pair two, three becomes two, four.

44
00:03:04.223 --> 00:03:08.642
So the outgoing action swaps zero with one and two with four.

45
00:03:08.642 --> 00:03:12.483
That is exactly the label stored in this certificate.

46
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:18.477
Because this overstrand action is its own inverse, the relation is B, then A, then B.

47
00:03:18.477 --> 00:03:21.711
This is one explicit check, not the entire proof.

48
00:03:21.711 --> 00:03:27.123
The saved certificate passes the corresponding relation at all thirteen crossings.

49
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.481
Now put a value, zero or one, at each dot.

50
00:03:30.481 --> 00:03:34.528
Ask which assignments stay unchanged when a shuffle acts.

51
00:03:34.528 --> 00:03:38.503
If zero and one are swapped, their values must be equal.

52
00:03:38.503 --> 00:03:42.620
Otherwise the assignment changes when we perform the swap.

53
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.389
For our double transposition, the values at zero and one must agree, and the values at

54
00:03:48.389 --> 00:03:53.026
two and three must agree. These are two independent equality requirements.

55
00:03:53.026 --> 00:03:56.848
The other four values remain unrestricted by this one action.

56
00:03:57.233 --> 00:04:01.011
Think of eight switches you can initially set independently.

57
00:04:01.011 --> 00:04:03.906
The first equality ties two switches together.

58
00:04:03.906 --> 00:04:06.299
The second ties another pair together.

59
00:04:06.299 --> 00:04:11.587
Six independent choices remain. Algebra calls this a rank difference of two, but the

60
00:04:11.587 --> 00:04:13.601
picture is just paired equality.

61
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.647
Every meridian for this knot gives the same kind of action, possibly with the dots renamed.

62
00:04:20.647 --> 00:04:25.322
Reversing a meridian changes nothing for these two-swap actions.

63
00:04:25.322 --> 00:04:31.678
So each possible meridian contributes at most two independent requirements, however you

64
00:04:31.678 --> 00:04:32.408
choose it.

65
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.465
Now apply the four saved seed actions.

66
00:04:35.465 --> 00:04:38.269
Draw a connection for each swapped pair.

67
00:04:38.269 --> 00:04:44.370
An assignment fixed by all four actions must give equal values across every connection.

68
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.016
Watch how the separate groups of dots join together.

69
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.348
After all four actions, every dot is connected to every other by a path.

70
00:04:53.348 --> 00:04:58.845
Equality travels along those paths. All eight values must therefore be the same.

71
00:04:58.845 --> 00:05:02.968
There are only two fixed assignments: all zeros or all ones.

72
00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:07.653
We began with eight independent choices and ended with just one.

73
00:05:07.653 --> 00:05:10.867
That is seven independent equality requirements.

74
00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:16.158
The full checked action is transitive, meaning every dot can reach every other.

75
00:05:16.158 --> 00:05:19.975
This connectedness is all the lower-bound argument needs.

76
00:05:20.367 --> 00:05:24.607
Suppose three meridians could generate the whole knot group.

77
00:05:24.607 --> 00:05:30.686
Their actions would have exactly the same common fixed assignments as the full action.

78
00:05:30.686 --> 00:05:35.775
But each supplies at most two requirements, so three supply at most six.

79
00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:39.517
The actual action needs seven. Six cannot reach seven.

80
00:05:39.517 --> 00:05:44.977
This contradiction rules out every generating set of three meridians, not just the three

81
00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:49.631
most obvious strands in the picture. At least four meridians are necessary.

82
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.134
Since bridge number is at least meridional rank, at least four bridges are necessary

83
00:05:55.134 --> 00:06:00.513
too. That is the hard lower bound. We still need the other direction: a certificate that

84
00:06:00.513 --> 00:06:02.408
four bridges really are enough.

85
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.362
Return to the actual diagram. Mark seed arcs zero, three, seven and four with four colours.

86
00:06:09.362 --> 00:06:15.852
Here a colour records information propagated from a seed; it does not represent a physical

87
00:06:15.852 --> 00:06:18.664
change to the string or a new crossing.

88
00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:24.156
The local rule is simple. If the overstrand and one understrand are already coloured,

89
00:06:24.156 --> 00:06:28.435
you may colour the other understrand with the known understrand colour.

90
00:06:28.435 --> 00:06:32.593
The crossing relation explains why its information is now determined.

91
00:06:32.967 --> 00:06:35.660
The saved certificate gives nine such moves.

92
00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:40.189
We replay those exact moves here, reaching every one of the thirteen arcs.

93
00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:45.759
A published theorem about these colourings turns a four-seed colouring into a bridge-number

94
00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:46.983
upper bound of four.

95
00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:52.837
Combine the two directions. Four is at most meridional rank, which is at most bridge

96
00:06:52.837 --> 00:06:57.396
number, which is at most four. Every quantity is forced to equal four.

97
00:06:57.396 --> 00:07:00.783
We did not infer this by counting diagram crossings.

98
00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:06.042
The same reasoning works for twenty four knots using double transpositions.

99
00:07:06.042 --> 00:07:11.372
One more, thirteen n four thousand six hundred and forty five, uses a three-cycle.

100
00:07:11.372 --> 00:07:17.223
A three-cycle rotates three dots and imposes two equalities; it is not two disjoint swaps.

101
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.688
That gives twenty five determinations relative to the pinned list of two hundred and

102
00:07:22.688 --> 00:07:28.139
ninety three names. Subtracting leaves two hundred and sixty eight entries in that ledger.

103
00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:32.864
The release and snapshot dates matter: this is a precisely defined comparison.

104
00:07:33.233 --> 00:07:36.057
There are twenty seven certificates altogether.

105
00:07:36.057 --> 00:07:41.345
The extra two confirm values for trefoil cables that already follow from older theorems.

106
00:07:41.345 --> 00:07:46.572
They are excluded from the twenty five additions, but remain in the mechanical residual

107
00:07:46.572 --> 00:07:49.937
ledger. So that ledger is not a list of proven unknowns.

108
00:07:50.333 --> 00:07:53.678
Keep three steps in mind. Check the crossing actions.

109
00:07:53.678 --> 00:07:56.959
Use eight connected dots to demand seven equalities.

110
00:07:56.959 --> 00:08:01.376
Then combine the four-meridian lower bound with a four-seed colouring.

111
00:08:01.376 --> 00:08:07.307
Two independent exact checkers replay all twenty seven certificates, including the upper-bound

112
00:08:07.307 --> 00:08:07.685
moves.

113
00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:13.063
The contribution is the twenty five table determinations and their certificates.

114
00:08:13.063 --> 00:08:15.748
Finite permutation methods already existed.

115
00:08:15.748 --> 00:08:21.244
A separate audit found no overlap with three earlier Coxeter targets, but broader family

116
00:08:21.244 --> 00:08:26.115
theorems and uncatalogued earlier determinations remain a priority limitation.

117
00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:32.679
This does not settle every thirteen-crossing knot, or the general conjecture that meridional

118
00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:38.523
rank always equals bridge number. It gives exact answers for these named examples, with

119
00:08:38.523 --> 00:08:44.299
a replayable chain of reasoning. The paper and editable evidence accompany this video.

120
00:08:44.299 --> 00:08:46.516
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
